steps.body:
This idea explains a surprising number of real situations, and questions like to use them.

If an animal doubles in every dimension:

- its **skin area** goes up **4 times**, so it loses heat about 4 times as fast;
- its **mass** goes up **8 times**, since mass follows volume.

So the bigger animal has **twice as much body per unit of skin**, which is why large animals lose heat more slowly and small ones must eat constantly.

The same reasoning explains why:

- a scale model of a bridge is far stronger for its size than the real one, because strength depends on the **cross-sectional area** ($k^2$) while weight depends on **volume** ($k^3$);
- a large tin of paint is better value than a small one, if the price follows volume while the label follows area;
- an ice cube melts faster than a block of ice of the same shape, because it has more surface for its volume.

In each case the reasoning is the same: **area and volume scale differently, so their ratio changes with size.** That last sentence is what an examiner wants to see written down.
